## Proposed answer:
To enforce BGP sessions with a specific neighbor to only use 2-byte ASN format, you need to disable 4-byte ASN support at the neighbor level. Here’s the CLI configuration:

```
configure router bgp group <group-name> neighbor <neighbor-ip>
    no asn4-capability
exit
```

Replace `<group-name>` with your BGP group name and `<neighbor-ip>` with the IP address of the specific neighbor. The `no asn4-capability` command ensures the session will only use 2-byte ASN format.
